specs.structured_output:
Only two `response_format.type` values are documented/accepted: `"json_schema"` and `"text"`. **`"json_object"` (schema-less JSON mode) is explicitly rejected with an HTTP 400** — confirmed independently across several real-world bug reports, not just one: "LM Studio rejects request with response_format set to json_object" (https://github.com/lfnovo/esperanto/issues/46), "response_format 'json_object' rejected by LM Studio (expects 'json_schema' or 'text')" (https://github.com/alex-on-ai/WebReaper/issues/265), "Support for json_object response_format" open feature request (https://github.com/lmstudio-ai/lmstudio-bug-tracker/issues/189), and a Microsoft Agents-for-net report of the same (https://github.com/microsoft/Agents-for-net/issues/55).

Exact documented shape for the supported mode (https://github.com/lmstudio-ai/docs/blob/main/1_developer/3_openai-compat/structured-output.md , fetched verbatim):
```
"response_format": {
  "type": "json_schema",
  "json_schema": {
    "name": "joke_response",
    "strict": "true",
    "schema": { "type": "object", "properties": {"joke": {"type": "string"}}, "required": ["joke"] }
  }
}
```
Note the docs literally show `"strict": "true"` as a JSON *string*, not a boolean — possibly a docs typo vs. OpenAI's boolean convention; I could not confirm which LM Studio actually enforces, so treat `strict`'s exact type as version/doc-precision-uncertain. The docs state no `json_object`-without-schema mode exists on this page at all.

Response still comes back as a string in `choices[0].message.content` that the caller must `json.loads()` — same as Runesmith's `parse_json_answer` already expects.

Engine backing: GGUF models use llama.cpp's grammar-based constrained sampling; MLX models (Apple Silicon) use the Outlines library. Documented limitation: "Not all models are capable of structured output, particularly LLMs below 7B parameters" — verify per-model via its README. SDK-level structured output also exists via the TypeScript SDK (Zod) and Python SDK (Pydantic/msgspec), but those are separate from the raw REST surface Runesmith uses. (All from the structured-output.md source above.)
